.TH TKBENCH 1
.SH NAME
tkbench \- time a widget repaint
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B tkbench
[
.B -v
]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Tkbench
measures what it costs to redraw a widget, under each of
.IR style (9)'s
two looks.
The question it was written for is whether
.I libtk
should be drawing on the GPU: it draws with
.I memdraw
which is scalar C, and nothing in the tree had ever timed it, so
"fast enough" was an inference from what the work looks like rather than
a number.
.PP
It builds a toplevel offscreen, over an ordinary image rather than the
display's screen, the same way
.IR tkofftest (1)
and
.IR tkgallery (1)
do.
.B "style \fIthe style it already has\fP"
is the repaint trigger:
.I tkstylecmd
marks every widget dirty and calls
.IR tkupdate ,
so one call is one full-tree repaint, which is the thing being measured
and is otherwise awkward to provoke.
.PP
.B -v
prints the repetition count and elapsed time behind each figure.
.PP
.B -s
puts the window on the display's real screen instead of an offscreen
image, so that every repaint ends in a Metal upload and present.
Offscreen there is no present at all, which is what makes the default
figures drawing-only.
.PP
.B -y
reports how often the display asks for a frame, by reading
.B #i/draw/vsync
\(em where a read blocks until the next frame is wanted \(em and counting
for three seconds.
Nothing is drawn.
Since
.I emu
presents the 2-D screen only from that callback, the rate this prints is
the rate the screen can update at, and it is measurable without seeing
the screen: a useful property, because
.I readpixels
returns
.B "00 00 00"
for anything on the display under the Metal present path, so looking is
the one thing that cannot be done.
.PP
.B -i
repaints slowly, 200 times at 25ms apart, leaving the processor idle
between frames.
It is for reading the present timers in
.B emu
with
.BR INFERNO_METAL_STATS ,
and it is not optional for that purpose \(em see BUGS.
.SS The number that means something is the slope
For each widget it reports the repaint time of a toplevel holding a few
of them, the same holding many, and
.B each
\(em the difference divided by the difference in count.
Only
.B each
is free of the fixed cost of a repaint, which is about 60
.RI \(mc s
and swamps a single widget.
.PP
The first version of this subtracted an empty toplevel instead, and the
figures were noise: the scaling control reported one widget's cost at
336937 per cent of an eighth of eight of them, which is a measurement
saying it is worthless.
A slope between two counts cancels the fixed cost without relying on two
separately measured runs agreeing.
.SH EXAMPLE
.EX
emu\-cocoa \-r. /dis/sh.dis \-c tkbench
.EE
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/cmd/tkbench.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR style (9),
.IR tkgallery (1),
.IR tkofftest (1)
.SH BUGS
It needs a window system to allocate a display, so it runs under
.I emu\-cocoa
and not
.IR emu\-g ,
even though it draws nothing on the screen.
.PP
The default figures time the DRAWING only.
What it costs to get those pixels onto the display is reported separately
by
.B emu
under
.BR INFERNO_METAL_STATS ,
as
.B METALTIME
and
.BR METALSPLIT .
On an idle machine a present of a 900\(mu700 screen costs about 200
.RI \(mc s,
of which roughly 150 is work, 120 of that the upload, and the rest a
wait for a drawable.
.PP
THOSE FIGURES NOW DESCRIBE ONLY THE OLD PATH, and are reached with
.B INFERNO_NOVSYNC
set.
Since the display began asking for frames rather than being told when a
program flushes, the timers report nothing at all: for a workload that
makes 179 presents with
.B INFERNO_NOVSYNC
set, the same workload without it makes fewer than thirty, which is the
count the counters print at.
The present path that runs by default is therefore NOT measured by
anything here, and no number in this page should be read as describing
it.
.PP
THOSE TIMERS ARE WALL CLOCK ON THE APPKIT THREAD, and that is a trap
rather than a detail.
Run while this benchmark is saturating the processor, the same present
reports 23
.IR ms ,
ninety times larger, because the thread spent that time waiting to be
scheduled rather than working.
A figure taken under load measures the load.
.B -i
exists to avoid it, and any present number quoted without it is wrong.
.PP
A widget packed outside the window might be clipped and never drawn,
which would make an expensive widget look cheap.
Nothing detects that automatically; the two counts are reported beside
the slope so that a figure which does not grow with the count is visible
as such.
.PP
The clock is
.I sys->millisec
and so has millisecond resolution.
Each figure is a loop run for at least 600ms to get below that, which
makes a full run take about half a minute.
